      "content": "No worries, I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the house stuff.\n\nSo, when it comes to releasing the funds from the escrow account, it's usually triggered by a series of events, like the inspection report, appraisal, and title search. Once all these documents are in order, the escrow agent will review them and verify that everything is in place.\n\nIt's like a secret password that unlocks the treasure chest. The password is the final inspection report, and once it's verified, the escrow agent will release the funds to the seller, and the transaction will be complete.\n\nIn most cases, the escrow agent will also hold back a small portion of the funds, usually 1-3% of the purchase price, as a contingency fund. This is like a goblin's stash, set aside for any unexpected expenses or issues that might arise during the transaction.\n\nThe escrow agent will then disburse the remaining funds to the seller, and the property will be transferred to your name. It's a bit like a magical transfer of ownership, where the funds are waved goodbye, and the property is yours to keep.",
